WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:05.470
Film Courage: One of the techniques or topics
regarding dialogue interests me that remember

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:11.310
you talking about was dumping dialogue versus
just kind of like “real dialogue.”

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.430
So dumping of information?

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:14.490
I don’t know if that is correct.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:15.490
It’s all at once.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:16.490
It’s not breathing.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:17.490
So dumping of information?

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.070
But sort of like giving everybody everything
they need right then and there.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:20.070
It’s too much.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:21.070
It’s not breathing.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.070
It’s not natural.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.319
It doesn’t evolve.

00:00:23.319 --> 00:00:29.820
So how can someone slow down without giving
the reader or the audience too much information?

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:35.480
Karl Iglesias: Well, the first thing you have
to understand about dialogue is that it’s

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.870
got to be connected to the character’s desire
line in the scene.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:46.519
In other words if the character has an objective,
an intention in the scene, the dialogue has

00:00:46.519 --> 00:00:48.770
to match that intention.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:53.960
And what I see a lot of times, especially
with exposition and information, is that a

00:00:53.960 --> 00:01:01.570
character is saying all that information and
you can tell that it’s really the writer’s

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:02.570
objective.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:08.290
In other words, the writer wants the audience
to know this information because they think

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:11.750
the audience need to know this, and this and
this, so the character are going to say this,

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:12.821
and this, and this.

00:01:12.821 --> 00:01:18.180
And that doesn’t work because scenes and
dialogue are about the character’s intention

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.720
in the scene.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:27.770
Everything a character says is matched to
what that desire line is.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.170
So anything that doesn’t belong to that
doesn’t fit.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:35.250
And you can tell whether it’s the writer
wanting the audience to know that information

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:45.330
or if it’s part of the character.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:52.410
I am trying to think of an example…So write
the dialogue, first draft, do you whatever

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:53.410
you want to do.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.550
Don’t worry about that.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:02:01.640
But when you re-read the dialogue, ask yourself
for every line “Why is that character saying

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:02.640
this?

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.090
Why is that character saying that specific
line?”

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.410
Most of the time it will be because they’re
responding to or they’re reacting to something.

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:09.410
That’s fine.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:13.590
But a lot of times you will find that a character
is saying something because you want the character

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:18.700
to say that, not because the character wants
something in that scene at a specific time.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:24.630
So you’ve got to know what the character
wants and then every line of dialogue is a

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:30.810
strategy for that character to get what they
want and if that dialogue is not that, then

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.510
you rewrite the line until it fits.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:39.340
Now in exposition, there are ways to make
sure the exposition in your dialogue matches

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:40.780
the objective.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:51.650
For example, if I’m suspected of murder
and you’re the detective who is asking me

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:52.650
questions, right?

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.790
And telling me “Where were you?” and I
didn’t commit the murder, well whatever

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:01.670
I’m going to say is my desire to make you
think I didn’t do the murder.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:03.400
There is a reason why I’m saying that line.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.400
I didn’t do it.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:05.890
I was at the movies.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:09.590
Saying at the movies is exposition but its
connected to my desire to convince you that

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:11.170
I was not the killer.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:18.230
You see, so the exposition matches the objective
of the character.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:19.380
So there are ways to do this.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:26.150
There are techniques to make sure the exposition
is settled, you know, part of conflict, part

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:27.160
of your desire line.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.680
There is a lot of stuff you can do.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:31.380
But that is the lesson you really have to
know.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.430
That is the most important thing.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:37.530
Make sure you know what every character wants
in the scene, make sure that what they say

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:39.640
matches that objective.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:48.380
So I always bring up David Mamet, who is genius
at dialogue and he said once “Nobody says

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:53.370
anything unless they want something.”

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:57.050
So unless you want something, nobody says
anything.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:02.120
Nobody is going to say “Hey, I’m a trustworthy
character.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.120
Right?

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.290
If somebody said that to you, you’re going
to think “Whoa…why is he telling me this?”

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:10.290
There is always a reason, right?

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:14.110
And if we know what the reason is in the movie,
if you know what the character wants, you

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:18.469
can kind of figure out what the character
wants, it’s more interesting than somebody

00:04:18.469 --> 00:04:20.540
dumping exposition.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:24.879
Characters who just dump exposition, it’s
just boring because it’s not connected to

00:04:24.879 --> 00:04:26.349
desire.

00:04:26.349 --> 00:04:27.990
Even characters who disagree with each other.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:33.370
Even though that might be kind of interesting
because there is some tension there, if we

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:35.969
don’t know what they want it’s going to
be boring.

00:04:35.969 --> 00:04:40.680
So you see this a lot with amateur scenes,
like “Oh, I need conflict so my character

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.740
is just going to argue” and you have people
arguing back and forth.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.770
It doesn’t mean anything.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:46.879
Right?

00:04:46.879 --> 00:04:47.879
Because we don’t know what they want.

00:04:47.879 --> 00:04:53.419
That’s what makes something interesting
is intention and the obstacle to that.

00:04:53.419 --> 00:04:57.909
Film Courage: We talked earlier about dumbing
things down, which this is a different way

00:04:57.909 --> 00:05:03.889
of talking about it, but let’s suppose it’s
a doctor speaking to another doctor but they’re

00:05:03.889 --> 00:05:04.889
not going to say…

00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:07.879
Karl Iglesias: But they’re not going to
use doctor lingo?

00:05:07.879 --> 00:05:08.879
Film Courage: Exactly!

00:05:08.879 --> 00:05:11.509
And they may say something that is grammatically
incorrect.

00:05:11.509 --> 00:05:13.740
They may use slang.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:14.819
They may say “Um.”

00:05:14.819 --> 00:05:15.819
Right?

00:05:15.819 --> 00:05:16.860
It’s not going to be perfect.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.809
Karl Iglesias: It depends on the character.

00:05:19.809 --> 00:05:22.539
I like to talk a lot about contrast.

00:05:22.539 --> 00:05:27.150
If we have two characters in a scene, it’s
always more interesting if the characters

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:29.529
are contrasted.

00:05:29.529 --> 00:05:34.120
So if a character is speaking in a formal
way, so someone who is an academic or a professor,

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:39.699
or a doctor speaking in a formal way, make
sure that the person that he’s interacting

00:05:39.699 --> 00:05:46.469
with speaks more slangy or in a more contracted
way because then you are going to create that

00:05:46.469 --> 00:05:47.469
contrast.

00:05:47.469 --> 00:05:51.580
Just like in painting, if you want to highlight
the color blue, you surround it with the color

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:52.580
yellow.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:53.580
Right?

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:54.580
It’s opposite.

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:55.599
So it’s the same thing with characters.

00:05:55.599 --> 00:06:00.479
A lot of the techniques in the dialogue chapter
in the book are about individuality.

00:06:00.479 --> 00:06:04.039
How do you create individual voices because
having all the characters sound the same are

00:06:04.039 --> 00:06:06.020
a very common problem with dialogue.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.809
It’s usually the writer’s voice.

00:06:07.809 --> 00:06:15.900
And (Aaron) Sorkin has actually been criticized
that all his characters sound the same.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:24.080
But when I talk about his dialogue and show
clips and the actual script I show that he

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.219
actually does individualize and the characters
do sound different.

00:06:28.219 --> 00:06:32.129
So it’s an unfair criticism.

00:06:32.129 --> 00:06:34.289
But you have to do that.

00:06:34.289 --> 00:06:42.740
If there is some difference in character speech
or cadence or contractions versus stilted

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:46.349
writing, grammatical, correct writing.

00:06:46.349 --> 00:06:50.539
Film Courage: What if the characters speak
to themselves?

00:06:50.539 --> 00:06:51.539
What if they’re thinking out loud?

00:06:51.539 --> 00:06:52.940
What if they’re looking in the mirror?

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:54.539
What if they’re berating themselves, driving
and talking to themselves?

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:55.539
Karl Iglesias: That’s fine.

00:06:55.539 --> 00:06:56.570
It’s really a character thing.

00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:57.570
As long as you keep it short.

00:06:57.570 --> 00:07:01.689
The problem that I see (with a character talking
to themselves) is it’s too long.

00:07:01.689 --> 00:07:04.889
You know then we kind of wonder “Where’s
the conflict?

00:07:04.889 --> 00:07:06.130
What’s happening here?”

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:10.770
I mean it might be interesting, but even something
that is really interesting for too long, we’ll

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:13.849
tend to disengage an audience after awhile.

00:07:13.849 --> 00:07:16.110
So you can do it, just don’t do it for too
long.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:17.110
Question for the Viewers: Do you have trouble
writing dialogue?

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:17.116
What helps you write better dialogue?

